1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,703
How can a robot move through confined three-dimensional spaces?

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:11,600
It must coordinate its route, posture,
and the body motion it can actually execute.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,996
Our method keeps a shared clearance core.

4
00:00:15,307 --> 00:00:21,600
Each platform supplies body geometry,
response bounds, and native command limits.

5
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,023
A lower posture command does not instantly lower the robot.

6
00:00:27,573 --> 00:00:32,330
Queued commands and finite response determine when the body changes.

7
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:38,725
We predict an enclosing cylinder whose
height and radius follow that execution.

8
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:44,831
Checking predicted poses alone can miss contact between them.

9
00:00:45,381 --> 00:00:51,141
We enlarge endpoint envelopes using bounded motion and shape-change rates.

10
00:00:51,691 --> 00:00:58,219
Their union covers the intervening body sweep,
so clearance is checked over the entire interval.

11
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:04,385
The filter coordinates motion and posture, then verifies dispatch.

12
00:01:04,856 --> 00:01:12,600
The clearance guarantee requires valid coverage,
support, map, and timing bounds, with static obstacles.

13
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,833
The same core is demonstrated with Go2, Go2W, and G1.

14
00:01:19,383 --> 00:01:24,311
These recordings show three-dimensional
routes across different embodiments.

15
00:01:24,861 --> 00:01:30,216
Each platform retains its native locomotion and calibrated interface.

16
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:40,948
Across 360 Go2 simulation tasks, safe completion reaches 93.9 percent.

17
00:01:41,498 --> 00:01:47,557
Full-cycle latency is 13.1 percent lower than Rollout-CBF.

18
00:01:48,107 --> 00:01:55,488
On 180 common-safe tasks, traversal is
19.0 percent shorter than stop-lower-go.

19
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:08,511
On held-out Go2W and G1, frozen-core reuse
reduces adaptation time by 43.3 to 53.6 percent.

20
00:02:08,952 --> 00:02:12,600
Success stays within two percentage points of retuning.

21
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:20,049
On the Go2 EDU, 90 hardware trials yield 85 safe completions.

22
00:02:20,599 --> 00:02:28,919
The ninety-fifth percentile full-cycle latency
is 15.4 milliseconds on the onboard Orin NX.

23
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:40,140
These separate recordings show stair and
obstacle negotiation with pedestrian crossings.

24
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:46,429
They complement the gate benchmark;
its success count is not pedestrian-specific.

25
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:55,535
Execution-aware body envelopes connect motion and
posture for reusable three-dimensional clearance navigation.

